<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State Bottleneck</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1923 CE · capital Lorch</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Accidental mini-state in the Rhine valley, left unoccupied between French and American zones after the First World War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> of 1918 gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> bridgeheads on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, drawn as circles of 30 kilometres around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. The two circles left a narrow gap near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorch-am-rhein">Lorch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaub">Kaub</a>, cut off from unoccupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by hills, which became known from January 1919 as the Free State Bottleneck.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The area of some 17,000 people had no direct road or rail link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorch-am-rhein">Lorch</a>'s mayor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-pnischeck">Edmund Pnischeck</a>, headed an improvised administration. Locals smuggled goods across the hills, printed their own emergency money and, in a famous episode, hijacked a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> train to keep warm.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, French troops occupied the territory in February 1923, ending its separate existence. The area returned to German administration when the French occupation was later lifted.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-bottleneck.jpg" alt="Free State Bottleneck" width="320" height="210"><figcaption>Image: Skrattar du Fölora du · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Free_State_of_Bottleneck.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1923 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lorch</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edmund Pnischeck</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Freistaat Flaschenhals</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1921&amp;focus=free-state-bottleneck" aria-label="Free State Bottleneck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4859 437 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State Bottleneck lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5179 693L5300 622M5179 693L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5179 693h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5179 693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lorch. <a href="/atlas?year=1921&amp;focus=free-state-bottleneck">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
